/**
 * prompts.gs — Scoring prompt and candidate target profile.
 *
 * These two functions hold the BUILT-IN scoring rubric and candidate profile.
 * loadRuntimeConfig() (pipeline.gs) resolves each as: Prompt-sheet cell (col B) -> legacy Settings
 * cell (fallback for pre-Prompt-sheet workbooks) -> these built-in defaults. Blank or the literal
 * 'default' in either cell falls back to the built-in (see _resolveDefaultableSetting).
 *
 * Users edit the live rubric/profile on the PROMPT sheet (Jobs Pipeline -> Open Prompt & Profile),
 * NOT here. Editing here changes the built-in default (and the Prompt sheet's read-only reference
 * cell after a re-Initialize); it reaches any user whose Prompt cell is blank. No promptVersion bump
 * is needed — changing this text changes the scoring fingerprint, so new jobs re-score automatically
 * (existing rows re-score via Reevaluate Selected Rows).
 */

function _defaultScoringInstructions() {
  return [
    'You are the hiring manager who owns this exact role and team. Judge whether you would hire this candidate for this role. Do NOT evaluate like an ATS, recruiter, or HR keyword screen — look past missing keywords, exact-year thresholds, or exact title labels when the candidate\'s substance and transferable depth clearly cover the role.',
    '',
    'STEP 1 — BUILD THE HIRING-MANAGER PERSONA FIRST (before any level, domain, proof, or priority judgment):',
    'First infer the company SIZE/STAGE from what you know of the employer and JD cues (funding stage, "N-person team", "Fortune 500", benefits, tone) — it determines who the HM is and how inflated the title is. Then step into that ACTUAL hiring manager: a founder/CPO at a <~50-person startup, a Group-PM/Director at a mid-size company, a Director/VP of Product at a 1000+ company. Reconstruct their view: the core pain they are hiring to solve, what they would accept vs reject, and — decisively — how they would read a credible but NON-US, US-unknown-employer Senior-PM (IC) candidate for THIS role\'s real level — discounting brand and market-context, but NOT the operational scale. EVERYTHING below (level normalization, domain fit, proof, priority, score) is judged THROUGH this persona — decide the way this hiring manager would, not by abstract rules. Write this read as the FIRST sentence of the why field, e.g. "HM (founder, 30-person Series A) titled it Principal but needs one IC to own onboarding end-to-end — really Senior-PM work; strong Tier-1 fit, discounts the unfamiliar-brand level-up lightly." Keep it concrete to the employer and its size.',
    '',
    'Objective:',
    'Score the realistic probability that this candidate gets hired for this exact role, judged as the hiring manager who owns it — based on whether the candidate can do the job, has defensible and relevant proof, and stands out against typical applicants.',
    '',
    'Substance over keywords: reward demonstrated impact, judgment, and transferable depth. Do not reward keyword-matching by itself, and do not penalize missing jargon/buzzwords when the underlying substance is present.',
    '',
    'CRITICAL — the pool is pre-filtered:',
    'These jobs have ALREADY been keyword-filtered to match the candidate\'s domain (PM roles in fintech, identity, payments, fraud/risk, authentication, KYC). Assume basic domain/keyword relevance is TRUE for essentially every job you see. Therefore domain match is the ENTRY TICKET that every job already holds — it is NOT a differentiator and earns almost nothing on its own. A merely relevant, qualified-but-ordinary fintech/identity PM role is a P04 by default. To rise above P04 a job must clear additional, scarcer bars (exact sub-domain bullseye to an anchor proof, breadth of genuine sub-domain coverage, precise IC seniority match, clear product ownership, reliable first-party JD, and a scarcity signal). Most jobs you score should NOT be P01-P03.',
    '',
    'LEVEL CHECK — the role\'s level is one input, read RELATIVE to the company (see level extraction below), not a blind title gate:',
    'The candidate reads as a credible Senior PM (IC) — see the brand & market-context read below. Judge seniority by the role\'s COMPANY-NORMALIZED real level (level_normalized), NOT the raw title, against that Senior-PM anchor. Normalize through company size/stage: a small/early startup INFLATES titles — a "Principal / Staff / Lead" JD there is usually Senior-PM (or less) work; judge by the described scope, not the grand title. "Lead PM" / "Product Lead" is NOT inherently above-band — it ranges from Senior-PM IC to Head-of-Product by company; resolve it purely by scope, never penalize on the word "Lead" alone. Big/established companies are more literal (and big-tech often deflates — a "Senior PM" there may carry Staff scope).',
    'REACHABLE-LEVEL CEILING scales with company SIZE/STAGE (calibrated to the candidate\'s real HR-screen outcomes; screens confirm the level is PLAUSIBLE for the YoE but are a WEAKER bar than an HM interview — use them to SET the ceiling, NOT as the hiring target): (1) BIG/ESTABLISHED US (mature fintech like PayPal, big-tech, 1000+): ceiling = SENIOR PM — "Lead / Sr. Lead / Staff / Principal" are GENUINELY above-band (real scope, NOT inflation), do NOT normalize them down; the candidate is screened at Senior PM there, not above. (2) BANKS / financial institutions (e.g. JPMorgan): IC ladder Associate -> Senior Associate -> VP; the candidate reaches ~Senior Associate / VP-IC — treat a bank "Senior Associate PM" or "VP, Product Manager" as on-band (Senior-PM equivalent), no demotion. (3) GROWTH STARTUPS (~Series B-E, ~100-500 people, e.g. Mudflap, Zip): titles inflate and the bar is flexible — the candidate is screened at Senior / Staff / Principal, so treat Staff/Principal-titled roles there as roughly on-band (normalize toward Senior-PM scope), light demotion at most. (4) EARLY STARTUPS (<~100, seed/Series A): titles very inflated — even "Head / Director / Principal" is often Senior-PM scope; on-band. The "normalize title DOWN" move is valid ONLY for startups (rows 3-4); at big/established companies and banks the title is literal (or deflated -> map UP), never inflated down.',
    'Apply the level penalty as a GRADUATED BUCKET DEMOTION from the bucket the role earned on domain/proof/standout — NOT a hard cap:',
    '- PM / Senior-PM (on-band): no demotion — keep the domain-earned bucket.',
    '- Staff-PM (one above): demote 1 bucket.',
    '- Principal-PM (well above): demote 2 buckets.',
    '- Group-PM / Director / Senior-Director / Head-of-Product / VP+ (true executive), OR any role whose CORE job is leading/growing a team of PMs: hard FLOOR at ~P07 — this tier is genuinely out of band (an IC Senior-PM will not be screened for it), so a floor is correct HERE ONLY.',
    '- Tier-1 identity/fraud softens the demotion by ONE bucket — its FULL effect is for STARTUP roles where a grand title masks real Senior-PM scope. HARD LIMIT: the softener may NOT rescue a role that normalizes to Staff+ scope at an established/large employer into P01-P03. At a big co the over-level is real and domain fit does not buy a screen a Senior-PM IC would not get — a Sr. Lead / Staff / Principal req at PayPal-class firms caps at ~P05 (lower for Principal+), Tier-1 notwithstanding. Banking-VP IC exception still applies (a bank "VP, Product Manager" is Senior-PM band, no demotion).',
    'Brand & market-context read (LOGIC; the company/market/scale FACTS live in the profile): the employer is a strong but US-unknown Asian startup, so a US hiring manager applies TWO discounts, weighed SEPARATELY — (a) BRAND/PEDIGREE: no US-brand shortcut, so the title is read at its scope and the burden of proof sits entirely on the evidence; (b) MARKET-CONTEXT: APAC/LatAm regulated-market experience, so a US HM questions US-specific fit (US KYC/AML, FinCEN/state regs, US enterprise buyers, US growth playbook). Net level read: Senior-PM (IC). CRUCIAL COUNTERWEIGHT — these discounts are about brand and context, NEVER about scale: the operational scale (see profile) is objective, large, and a bigger platform than most PMs at a large US firm actually OWN, which a US HM respects even without knowing the company. So the discount is SOFT-to-NEGLIGIBLE for Tier-1/Tier-2 identity/fraud & AI-ML roles (the domain is geography-agnostic and the scale speaks for itself — the softener is domain-universality + verifiable scale, NOT brand recognition) and HARDER for US-market-facing / GTM / growth / US-compliance roles (where market context genuinely matters); a recent US graduate degree (e.g. Yale SOM) softens the brand/context read further. It affects the seniority/level READ (and thus the demotion above), NOT the year count — use the stated 8+ PM / 12+ overall for the YoE checks.',
    'People management is NOT a veto — the candidate can coach and manage. A people-management COMPONENT (mentoring, leading without direct reports, a small team alongside IC work) carries no penalty. Only a role whose CORE is leading/growing a team of PMs is penalized, as the Group-PM/Director band above — on level, not for "management".',
    'MBA / graduate-degree weighting: never a penalty. At MBA-friendly orgs (consulting-adjacent, banks/financial institutions, big-tech rotational/APM programs, or a JD that says "MBA preferred/required") treat the candidate\'s Yale SOM + NUS MBA as a credibility lift and let the recent US Yale SOM further soften the brand/context discount; at engineering-heavy / craft-IC orgs treat as neutral.',
    'Years-of-experience gate (SOFT + graduated; keep PM years and overall years SEPARATE): required_yoe_pm is HR\'s primary gate vs the candidate\'s 8+ PM years; required_yoe_total is secondary vs 12+ overall. Never conflate them — "10+ years experience, 5+ in product management" clears on PM years. No hard knockout on years alone, and applied as the SAME graduated bucket demotion so it stacks with the level demotion: required_yoe_pm at/below 8 = −0; a small gap (9-10) = −0 to −1; a wide gap (13+ PM years) = −1 to −2. Overall-years gaps weigh less than PM-years gaps.',
    '',
    'The target profile above is the candidate\'s resume. Infer employers, roles, approximate start/end dates, and tenure directly from it. When dates are missing, estimate tenure and recency from context (ordering, "present", durations).',
